#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199096} :activity/id #uuid "fca1ce31-a26b-5dce-bb5a-6769c343dcd5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049366 :topic/id #uuid "ef2fa709-af77-5fbb-a8d9-f92b628eeaba" :topic/math-academy-id 4422} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199569 :tutorial/content "Up to now, we've seen several grammatical constructions for implications and biconditionals. In this lesson, we'll review all those that are commonly used.\n\nThe implication $P \\Rightarrow Q$ has the following grammatical constructions:\n\n- If $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $P$ only if $Q$\n\n- $Q$ if $P$\n\n- $P$ implies $Q$\n\nFor example, \n\n$\\qquad$ **If** $4 \\mid n,$ **then** $2 \\mid n$\n\nis equivalent to the following:\n\n- $4 \\mid n$ **implies** $2 \\mid n$\n\n- $4 \\mid n$ **only if** $2 \\mid n$\n\n- $2 \\mid n,$ **if** $4 \\mid n$\n\nUsing these grammatical constructions correctly within a particular mathematical context can be tricky. It's important to go through each and understand why it's equivalent to $P\\Rightarrow Q.$ So, let's do that. We assume that $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is *true* in each case.\n\n- If $P,$ then $Q{:}$ If $P$ is true, then $Q$ *must* be true.\n\n- $P$ only if $Q{:}$ The *only* way that $P$ is true is if $Q$ is also true.\n\n- $Q$ if $P{:}$ $Q$ is *guaranteed* to be true if $P$ is true.\n\n- $P$ implies $Q.$ This is the same as \"If $P,$ then $Q.$\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "5ce16042-d1e9-5bdf-a5b6-0c6f71ddce4b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8289 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dc7b467a-0513-53be-be39-87ac3ff581c3" :step/math-academy-id 154105 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199097}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198282 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198180 :question/id #uuid "d20f2ac9-3190-5c58-a0a5-ddcd8c3f5817" :question/math-academy-id "e-13234"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0809c075-e1be-5d49-b1fe-5433114bcf16" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048574}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421152}] :knowledge-point/title "\"If-Then\", \"If\", \"Only If\", and \"Implies\""} :step/id #uuid "2f3e80d5-b0a8-5272-b1aa-9f9129558bb8" :step/math-academy-id 154106 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199098}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199593 :tutorial/content "Recall that, in terms of necessary and sufficient conditions, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{Sufficient}\\phantom{|}}_{P} \\Rightarrow \\underbrace{\\textrm{Necessary}}_{Q}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following equivalent grammatical constructions:\n\n- $P$ is **sufficient** for $Q$\n\n- For $Q,$ it is **sufficient** that $P$\n\n- $Q$ is **necessary** for $P$\n\n- For $P,$ it is **necessary** that $Q$\n\nLet's go back to the following true implication that we encountered in a previous lesson:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\big( \\, a > 0 \\, \\big)}_{\\large\\textrm{sufficient}} \n\\: \\Rightarrow \\: \n\\underbrace{\\big( \\, a^2 > 0 \\, \\big)}_{\\large\\textrm{necessary}}\n$$\n\nWe can use the following grammatical constructions in terms of sufficient conditions:\n\n- $a > 0$ is a *sufficient condition* for $a^2 > 0$\n\n- $a > 0$ is *sufficient* for $a^2 > 0$\n\n- For $a^2 > 0,$ it is *sufficient* that $a > 0$\n\nThese all mean the same thing, namely that $a > 0$ *guarantees* that $a^2 > 0.$\n\nFor necessary conditions, we have:\n\n- $a^2 > 0$ is a *necessary condition* for $a > 0$\n\n- $a^2 > 0$ is *necessary* for $a > 0$\n\n- For $a > 0,$ it is *necessary* that $a^2 > 0$\n\nThese all mean that if $a > 0$ it follows that $a^2 > 0,$ but $a^2 > 0$ *does not guarantee* that $a > 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f1f0c849-1dba-5a3c-acab-d874bfd0bd9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8353 :tutorial/title "Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"} :step/id #uuid "f7854e8a-298b-5869-a1f8-25d564311e6e" :step/math-academy-id 154109 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199099}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198283 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198187 :question/id #uuid "8ba22130-b8bb-55d0-83b0-5a3b89bcf876" :question/math-academy-id "e-13275"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c26aeaa-9252-5bd3-9ad6-0fc25ca6b03c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048577}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421154}] :knowledge-point/title "Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"} :step/id #uuid "8b79784e-6d7f-54b3-911a-d4be6f515464" :step/math-academy-id 154110 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199100}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199100 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199594 :tutorial/content "Implication $P \\Rightarrow Q$ also can be written using \"whenever\" and \"provided\" as follows:  \n\n- **Whenever** $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $Q$ **whenever** $P$\n\n- $Q,$ **provided** that $P$\n\nLet's again go through each one and convince ourselves that they're all equivalent to $P\\Rightarrow Q.$ Again, we assume that $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is *true* in each case.\n\n- **Whenever** $P,$ then $Q{:}$ Whenever $P$ is true, $Q$ is guaranteed to be true.\n\n- $Q$ **whenever** $P{:}$ $Q$ is guaranteed to be true whenever $P$ is true.\n\n- $Q,$ **provided** that $P{:}$ $Q$ is guaranteed to be true provided that $P$ is true.\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "4d7ce271-7f3d-51dc-8313-ac1448341658" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8354 :tutorial/title "Whenever and Provided That"} :step/id #uuid "09e5d0ff-56a1-531b-8c73-bf32f7aba54b" :step/math-academy-id 154113 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199101}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199101 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198284 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198188 :question/id #uuid "c776807d-efab-534b-b79c-582e4906bb4b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13276"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b24ae815-5706-5b65-bab8-2d67f8405c9c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421149}] :knowledge-point/title "\"Whenever\", and \"Provided That\""} :step/id #uuid "2322cf93-67c8-548c-b4ef-289711eb1fe0" :step/math-academy-id 154114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199102}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199595 :tutorial/content "The grammatical constructions for biconditionals $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$ are slightly more straightforward than for conditionals.\n\nWe've already seen the following constructions:\n\n- $P$ **if and only if** $Q$\n\n- $P$ is **necessary and sufficient** for $Q$\n\n- $P$ is **equivalent** to $Q$\n\nThe following constructions are also commonly used:\n\n- $P$ **iff** $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ **and conversely**\n\nHere, \"iff\" is simply shorthand for \"if and only if.\"\n\nThe construction \"If $P$ then $Q,$ and conversely\" is interesting. Recall that the converse of $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is $Q\\Rightarrow P.$ If $P\\Rightarrow Q$ and $Q\\Rightarrow P$ are both true, we have that $P$ implies $Q$ and $Q$ implies $P,$ i.e., $P\\Leftrightarrow Q.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "034ee104-6c2c-5faf-b260-a171e852673c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8355 :tutorial/title "Biconditionals"} :step/id #uuid "39caa146-84bc-5745-84e8-df160bccbda7" :step/math-academy-id 154117 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199103}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198185 :question/id #uuid "f4b354bf-6629-5574-9674-fdc3ca707b0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-13273"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c614609-dae7-5cde-a26a-52104d47ebf5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050743}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421155}] :knowledge-point/title "Biconditionals"} :step/id #uuid "1829f7f4-e5f0-5eae-a3d2-1fb0fa1141b5" :step/math-academy-id 154118}] :activity/title "Grammatical Constructions for Conditional Statements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198539}]}
